  "notes": "Standard Marathi (Maharashtra). No phonemic vowel length -- the script writes the Sanskrit length distinction but the language has lost it, so ⟨आ ई ऊ ए ओ⟩ are transcribed without a length mark and ⟨अ⟩ vs ⟨आ⟩ is a quality contrast [ə] vs [a]. ⟨ळ⟩ is the retroflex lateral flap [ɭ̆], a Marathi hallmark. ⟨र⟩ is a tap. ⟨ऋ⟩ is read [ɾu], not the Sanskrit syllabic [r̩] nor the Hindi [ɾɪ]. ⟨च ज झ⟩ carry both an alveolar and a palato-alveolar affricate series. Final schwa deletion is near-categorical but is blocked after a consonant cluster. Anusvara before a stop is a homorganic nasal consonant. Medial schwa deletion is deliberately not encoded: Marathi retains the medial schwa far more freely than Hindi does (Wikipedia, 'Schwa deletion in Indo-Aryan languages': 'comprehension of Marathi is not impeded if all schwas are retained'; 'propensity to pronounce it far more regularly than Hindi'), and a fully-retained pronunciation is well formed -- Hindi's categorical VC_CV rule would fire on अंकगणित, where the gold keeps both vowels. The gold retains 81% of the medial schwas this spec emits; the remainder is the trade-off's cost, taken deliberately rather than by oversight.",
