      "notes": "Word-final schwa deletion, restricted to the post-vocalic case. Marathi deletes the inherent vowel of a word-final consonant letter -- अंगण [əŋɡəɳ], अंकुश [əŋkuɕ] -- but, unlike Hindi, it KEEPS that vowel when deleting it would leave a word-final consonant cluster: शब्द is [ɕəbdə], not *[ɕəbd]. The condition is stated as preceded_by_phoneme = the vowels, which is the same statement read off the slot to the left: the schwa survives exactly when the preceding slot ends in a consonant, whether that consonant is written with a virama (अंतरिक्ष [ənt̪əɾikʂə]) or is the homorganic nasal an anusvara writes (अंक [əŋkə], अंत [ənt̪ə]). Requiring a preceding slot at all also protects a one-letter word, whose inherent vowel is its only nucleus (न [nə]). Sanskrit keeps the inherent vowel unconditionally and declares no such rule, which is why this lives in the Marathi data and not in the engine. (Masica, The Indo-Aryan Languages, 1991, on schwa deletion in modern Indo-Aryan; Wikipedia, 'Marathi phonology', for the Marathi cluster exception: 'it has conserved the schwas after consonant clusters in words like शब्द'.) The nasalization mark is deliberately absent from the list, so an anusvara always blocks the deletion. Before a stop it is a homorganic nasal; before a continuant it is a nasalized approximant, which the gold writes as a segment of its own (गांव, नांव, मांस all keep their final schwa there). Either way the preceding slot is consonantal and the cluster condition holds. This also sidesteps a limit of the rule language: the anusvara slot still reads as the bare nasalization mark when the schwa rules are evaluated, so a guard on the preceding slot could not tell the homorganic nasal MR_ANUSVARA_* produces from plain vowel nasalization."
