    {
      "id": "MR_ANUSVARA_VELAR",
      "phonemes": [
        "̃"
      ],
      "surface": "ŋ",
      "grapheme": [
        "ं"
      ],
      "followed_by_grapheme": [
        "क",
        "ख",
        "ग",
        "घ"
      ],
      "notes": "An anusvara before a stop writes the nasal of a nasal+stop cluster, not nasalization of the preceding vowel, and that nasal takes the stop's place of articulation: अंक [əŋkə], अंत [əntə], अंड [əɳɖə], आंबा [amba]. Only before a non-stop, or at a word edge, does the sign surface as vowel nasalization. The Marathi gold bears this out: the homorganic consonants outnumber the nasalized vowels by more than an order of magnitude (ŋ 166, n 962, m 781, ɳ 1361 against ẽ 5, ĩ 2, ũ 1, ə̃ 1 in 4872 words). (Masica, The Indo-Aryan Languages, 1991, on the anusvara and homorganic nasals in modern Indo-Aryan Devanagari orthography.)"
    },
    {
