    {
      "id": "MR_AFFRICATE_PALATAL_ts",
      "phonemes": [
        "ts"
      ],
      "surface": "tɕ",
      "followed_by_phoneme": [
        "i",
        "e",
        "æ",
        "j"
      ],
      "notes": "Marathi's defining split: ⟨च ज झ⟩ stand for an alveolar affricate series [t͡s d͡z d͡zʱ] as well as the palato-alveolar [t͡ɕ d͡ʑ d͡ʑʱ]. Before a front vowel or the glide [j] only the palato-alveolar member occurs; before a non-front vowel the choice is lexical (broadly, native vocabulary takes the alveolar series and Sanskrit or Perso-Arabic loans the palato-alveolar one), so it cannot be read off the spelling and both readings stay in the lattice there. The one-directional part is what this rule states, and the gold is categorical about it: before a front vowel [t͡ɕ] outnumbers [t͡s] 77 to 1 and [d͡ʑ] outnumbers [d͡z] 53 to 4. (Wikipedia, 'Marathi language', listing च as /t͡ɕə/ or /t͡sə/ and ज as /d͡ʑə/ or /d͡zə/; Dhongde & Wali, Marathi, John Benjamins, 2009 -- edition not consulted, the split is quoted at second hand.)"
